WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.945
a clear day, a crew member on a merchant ship sailing across the Caribbean Sea peers out at

2
00:00:11.965 --> 00:00:13.687
the horizon through his telescope.

3
00:00:14.687 --> 00:00:16.989
He spies another ship approaching quickly.

4
00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.672
A black flag is flying high on its mast.

5
00:00:21.353 --> 00:00:22.034
He shivers.

6
00:00:22.594 --> 00:00:25.096
It can only mean one thing: pirates!

7
00:00:26.377 --> 00:00:32.113
This is a familiar scene from numerous stories and legends, but there was a time when pirates were

8
00:00:32.153 --> 00:00:34.673
a real concern for merchants and other sailors.

9
00:00:36.074 --> 00:00:39.395
Rogues violently boarded ships and stole their precious cargo.

10
00:00:40.375 --> 00:00:45.477
Terrified victims gave up their wealth and hoped that the experience would not end in their deaths.

11
00:00:46.557 --> 00:00:48.918
Pirates were notorious for their viciousness.

12
00:00:50.418 --> 00:00:56.260
Piracy, the crime of robbery at sea, has occurred all over the world for more than three thousand

13
00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:03.234
years. One of the oldest documents describing pirates is an Egyptian inscription on a clay tablet.

14
00:01:04.275 --> 00:01:12.598
The report dates back to Pharaoh Echnaton (1350 BC) and mentions Mediterranean pirates attacking ships off the coast

15
00:01:12.658 --> 00:01:13.578
of North Africa.

16
00:01:14.939 --> 00:01:20.461
Ancient Greek merchants who traded with ports in the Middle East also suffered at the hands of pirates.

17
00:01:21.522 --> 00:01:26.924
From the ninth to the eleventh century, the Vikings terrorized the coasts of Western Europe as well.

18
00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:34.105
The most famous pirates, however, sailed the Mediterranean Sea during the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries.

19
00:01:35.126 --> 00:01:42.292
At that time, the Barbary pirates, who operated out of North Africa, frequently attacked ships traveling to and

20
00:01:42.332 --> 00:01:42.972
from Europe.

21
00:01:44.273 --> 00:01:48.236
Their territory extended as far north as the English Channel and Iceland.

22
00:01:49.057 --> 00:01:56.583
The unfortunate victims were often sailors on merchant ships carrying gold and other valuables, such as rum, sugar,

23
00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:57.284
and weapons.

24
00:01:58.524 --> 00:02:04.409
With booty like this, a lucky pirate could earn enough from just one successful robbery to retire!

25
00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:11.836
Therefore, the North African coast continued to be a pirate stronghold well into the nineteenth century.

26
00:02:12.877 --> 00:02:17.641
Booty, however, was not the only thing that the Barbary pirates were interested in stealing.

27
00:02:18.822 --> 00:02:25.227
These robbers were also known to attack naval vessels; they could take command and use these ships as

28
00:02:25.287 --> 00:02:25.688
their own.

29
00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:33.464
At the time, there were many warships on the seas because European nations, including England, France, and Spain,

30
00:02:33.884 --> 00:02:35.585
were often at war with one another.

31
00:02:36.866 --> 00:02:42.769
Losing important ships to pirates was harmful to the military, so some governments decided that it would be

32
00:02:42.829 --> 00:02:46.151
better to have the robbers as friends rather than foes.

33
00:02:47.332 --> 00:02:50.474
So, they actually began to employ them to capture enemy ships.

34
00:02:51.594 --> 00:02:57.394
These new kinds of pirates, called privateers, were allowed to keep a large portion of the cargo they

35
00:02:57.454 --> 00:02:59.535
stole without facing punishment.

36
00:03:00.536 --> 00:03:07.621
In fact, the most famous privateer, Sir Francis Drake, was even made second-in-command of an entire fleet in

37
00:03:07.661 --> 00:03:10.984
the English navy to thank him for his devotion to the Queen.

38
00:03:12.365 --> 00:03:19.049
Inspired by the success of the privateers and encouraged by the lure of riches, other groups of wandering

39
00:03:19.109 --> 00:03:22.412
pirates began to harass the Spanish colonies in the New World.

40
00:03:23.504 --> 00:03:30.666
These pirates, called buccaneers, sailed in the Caribbean Sea and worked for the English government, protecting new trade

41
00:03:30.726 --> 00:03:33.547
routes and fighting the enemies of the English territories.

42
00:03:34.907 --> 00:03:40.969
Perhaps the most notorious buccaneer was Blackbeard (1680―1718).

43
00:03:42.129 --> 00:03:48.771
Born Edward Teach, this Englishman earned the nickname Blackbeard because he wore a thick black beard that covered

44
00:03:48.891 --> 00:03:50.412
almost his entire face.

45
00:03:51.836 --> 00:03:57.700
During his reign in the Caribbean, he was reported to have frightened his victims by putting gunpowder and

46
00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.902
matches into his hair and lighting them during battles.

47
00:04:02.123 --> 00:04:08.927
In addition, he intimidated people because he was big, wore two swords at his waist, and strapped pistols

48
00:04:08.987 --> 00:04:10.428
and knives across his chest.

49
00:04:11.949 --> 00:04:15.712
The sight of Blackbeard was enough to make most sailors surrender without a fight.

50
00:04:16.776 --> 00:04:21.960
If they resisted, however, he would maroon the crew on a nearby island and burn their ship.

51
00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:27.943
Like Blackbeard, most pirates scared their victims into retreating or surrendering.

52
00:04:28.884 --> 00:04:32.787
First, they spent several weeks searching for a suitable ship to attack.

53
00:04:33.787 --> 00:04:39.391
Then, when one was sighted by a lookout, the pirates would send up a Jolly Roger to warn

54
00:04:39.411 --> 00:04:40.832
their target that they were coming.

55
00:04:41.844 --> 00:04:47.488
The Jolly Roger, the traditional flag of the American and European pirates, was made with either red or

56
00:04:47.548 --> 00:04:52.032
black cloth, and a skull and crossbones was stitched on with white thread.

57
00:04:53.193 --> 00:04:58.357
This design originates from the symbol used to indicate the death of a sailor in a captain's log,

58
00:04:59.037 --> 00:05:03.681
and the sight of it flying on the mast of an oncoming ship struck fear into the hearts

59
00:05:03.721 --> 00:05:04.982
of everyone on the seas.

60
00:05:06.563 --> 00:05:12.722
Upon reaching the other ship, the pirates would throw over heavy rope ladders and climb on board, yelling

61
00:05:12.802 --> 00:05:15.124
loudly and shooting their pistols into the air.

62
00:05:16.224 --> 00:05:22.868
They wore strange and brightly colored costumes, and many had eye patches, peg legs, or hook hands as

63
00:05:22.928 --> 00:05:24.829
gruesome reminders of past battles.

64
00:05:25.949 --> 00:05:30.372
Only the bravest would fight them, and those captured would be forced to walk the plank.

65
00:05:31.312 --> 00:05:36.775
This was the pirates' favorite form of punishment for their prisoners; they were forced to walk down a

66
00:05:36.795 --> 00:05:42.370
board blindfolded until they fell off the edge into the sea, where they would certainly drown.

67
00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:48.292
Working on a pirate ship was a difficult life for young men and women, but it did have

68
00:05:48.352 --> 00:05:49.132
its advantages.

69
00:05:50.173 --> 00:05:51.593
Of course, it was profitable.

70
00:05:52.454 --> 00:05:58.136
Those who worked as buccaneers or privateers could also attain fairly high positions in the military.

71
00:05:59.117 --> 00:06:06.720
Furthermore, joining independent pirate ships provided an opportunity to live in a society unlike any other Western society

72
00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:07.360
at the time.

73
00:06:08.304 --> 00:06:12.825
Ships were ruled by pirate clans and operated as limited democracies.

74
00:06:13.906 --> 00:06:19.367
Leaders were elected, and the captain of a pirate ship was often a fierce fighter whom the crew

75
00:06:19.427 --> 00:06:20.487
could trust in battle.

76
00:06:21.488 --> 00:06:28.110
Many groups of pirates shared whatever booty they seized, distributing it among crew members according to their status.

77
00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:36.392
Pirates readily accepted outcasts from traditional society, and they were also known to free slaves from slave ships

78
00:06:36.852 --> 00:06:38.754
and welcome them into the pirate fold.

79
00:06:39.915 --> 00:06:44.278
None of these benefits, however, lessened the dangers of being professional thieves.

80
00:06:45.419 --> 00:06:51.444
Piracy waned following the development of the steam engine and the subsequent growth of the British and American

81
00:06:51.504 --> 00:06:53.846
naval forces in the late eighteenth century.

82
00:06:54.967 --> 00:07:03.234
The increased size of merchant vessels, improvements in communication technology, and regular patrols of most ocean highways helped

83
00:07:03.294 --> 00:07:05.036
reduce the number of pirates further.

84
00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:12.924
Eventually, governments stopped employing them; as most islands and land areas of the world came under the control

85
00:07:12.964 --> 00:07:16.466
of governments, they could no longer serve as pirate havens.

86
00:07:17.746 --> 00:07:25.470
Nonetheless, stories of pirates and their exploits endure because they represent an enticing image of wealth and adventure.

87
00:07:26.551 --> 00:07:35.436
So, the colorful clothes, frightening symbols, and hair-raising tales associated with pirates have stimulated people's imaginations for centuries.

88
00:07:36.436 --> 00:07:39.238
Some tales are true, and some are pure fiction.

89
00:07:40.379 --> 00:07:45.824
The idea of buried treasure, for example, is mostly myth, but it may have started with Captain Kidd,

90
00:07:46.544 --> 00:07:49.747
a pirate who actually did try to hide his gold underground.

91
00:07:50.608 --> 00:07:56.752
However, Long John Silver, in the book Treasure Island, and Captain Hook, in The Adventures of Peter Pan,

92
00:07:57.113 --> 00:07:59.615
are both frightening, but fictional, pirates.

93
00:08:00.796 --> 00:08:05.239
The distinctive language of pirates, including phrases like "Yoho!

94
00:08:05.339 --> 00:08:08.250
Ahoy!" (Hey, you!), "Arr, matey!"

95
00:08:08.271 --> 00:08:10.473
(Hello!), and "Avast!"

96
00:08:10.493 --> 00:08:16.740
(Stop!) add to people's fascination with the lives of historical and fictional pirates alike.

97
00:08:17.621 --> 00:08:21.846
Pirates are the adventurous outlaws we love to hate and hate to love.
